---- QUESTIONS: BIOS ---- 
>> B1: What is the first instruction that gets executed?

ljmp   $0x3630,$0xf000e05b

>> B2: At which physical address is this instruction located?

[f000:fff0] or 0xFFFF0

>> B3: Can you guess why the first instruction is like this?

The first instruction jumps to the address 0xf000e05b, which will continue to execute the rest of the BIOS code.

>> B4: What are the next three instructions?

1. 0xfe05b:	cmpw   $0xffc8,%cs:(%esi)
2. 0xfe062:	jne    0xd241d076
3. 0xfe066:	xor    %edx,%edx

---- QUESTIONS: BOOTLOADER ---- 
>> B5: How does the bootloader read disk sectors? In particular, what BIOS interrupt
is used?

The bootloader can read disk sectors using the BIOS interrupt 0x13, which we can see being used in the read_sector function.

>> B6: How does the bootloader decides whether it successfully finds the Pintos kernel?

It reads the partition table on each system hard disk and scans for a partition of type 0x20, which is the type that is used for a Pintos kernel. It uses this specific instruction to check: cmpb $0x20, %es:4(%si)

>> B7: What happens when the bootloader could not find the Pintos kernel?

If the bootloader can not find the Pintos kernel, it will call "puts" and print "Not found". Then, it will call the BIOS interrupt 0x18 to create a software interrupt and notify BIOS the boot failed.

>> B8: At what point and how exactly does the bootloader transfer control to the Pintos kernel?

If the bootloader can succesffuly find the pintos kernel, it will run load_kernel. Once all of the sectors of the kernel
are loaded, the control will be transferred to the Pintos kernel. This is done by using the start
address out of the ELF header and converting it from a  32-bit linear address into a 16:16 segment:offset address for
real mode, then jumping to the converted address.

